If the player's in a pit, he can't see the leocrotta.  No ESP, and he's
not blind, so the telepathy doesn't kick in, either.  So that last line
(identifying the q) can't be a message from the game, though that
doesn't change much.  It's been more than one turn since player fell
in, since he decided to put on some boots.  The only reason to put on
boots while in a pit is if you're hoping for levitation or jumping;
unfortunately, they're water walking, but the player doesn't know that.
 The player also doesn't know about the leocrotta approaching (if he
saw it in the room before falling in the pit, he wouldn't have taken
the time to put on the boots).

So, player's first move is going to be attempting to #jump.  Luckily,
getting "You can't jump very far." doesn't take any game time.  Next,
player'll try to climb out of the pit; given that they don't know about
the leocrotta, they'll probably try advancing into the room (they've
clearly explored the tunnel to their left; though, if they know about
the other trap--ie., if it's not just there to round out the
information--they've also explored the current room, so the direction's
not as sure).

At this point, we have three possibilities: 1) Player is still stuck in
the pit, and the leocrotta is next to them (and probably gets a hit or
two in).  2) Player gets out, on the room side, and the leocrotta is
next to them.  3) Player gets out on the corridor side.

Case 1: If the leocrotta's gotten in some hits when it came up,
hitpoints may be low enough that prayer is a viable option.
Regardless, player needs to buy some time by throwing down an Elbereth;
if prayer's not an option and the E doesn't work on the first shot,
they're probably dead.  Otherwise, once the leocrotta turns to flee,
player should throw down some more E's for safety, zap the leocrotta
when it comes back in sight, and then try to climb out into the
corridor.  Once in the corridor, player is more or less safe--the
leocrotta will fall in the pit, giving player the opportunity to throw
the daggers at it to soften it up before engaging in some
hit-and-retreat combat.

Case 2: Player pauses to go change underwear.  Then, if the leocrotta
hit when it came up, again consider prayer, and throw down some E's.
Zap the leocrotta, and then consider options.  My preference (since
player can't get to the corridor now, because there's a pit in the way)
would be to try to lure the leocrotta through the other trap (unless
it's polymorph), chuck the daggers, and hit-and-retreat until the
problem's gone.

Case 3: the simplest option.  When the leocrotta falls in the pit,
throw the daggers.  Zap the wand, and then just hit-and-retreat until
done.

